      "message": "Yes, exactly. With the current version, `ext_deps` implies that dependency tests are generated when `plugin` is set. That makes the common case simple, but there is no explicit escape hatch for a plugin that has external dependencies but does not want these dependency tests.\n\nI think adding an opt-out flag would address that:\n\n```python\nskip_dependency_tests \u003d False\n```\n\nand then:\n\n```python\nif not skip_dependency_tests and ext_deps and plugin:\n    gerrit_plugin_dependency_tests(\n        plugin \u003d plugin,\n        name \u003d dependency_test_name,\n        allowlist \u003d dependency_test_allowlist,\n        overlap_against \u003d dependency_test_overlap_against,\n    )\n```\n\nThe intended behavior would be:\n\nFor the standard case with external dependencies:\n\n```python\ngerrit_plugin_tests(\n    srcs \u003d glob([\"src/test/java/**/*.java\"]),\n    ext_deps \u003d EXT_DEPS,\n    plugin \u003d \"oauth\",\n)\n```\n\ndependency tests are generated automatically.\n\nFor plugins without external dependencies:\n\n```python\ngerrit_plugin_tests(\n    srcs \u003d glob([\"src/test/java/**/*.java\"]),\n    plugin \u003d \"reviewers\",\n)\n```\n\ndependency tests are not generated.\n\nFor the exception case, where a plugin has external dependencies but wants to opt out:\n\n```python\ngerrit_plugin_tests(\n    srcs \u003d glob([\"src/test/java/**/*.java\"]),\n    ext_deps \u003d EXT_DEPS,\n    plugin \u003d \"foo\",\n    skip_dependency_tests \u003d True,\n)\n```\n\nI considered the inverse option, `enable_dependency_tests \u003d False`, but that would make the common case more verbose. Since `ext_deps` usually means there are dependencies worth checking, I think automatic generation with an explicit opt-out keeps the standard case simple while still allowing exceptions.\n\nWDYT?",
